  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Refining UI Considerations**\n\nI\'m focused on clean UI polish, leveraging the active `frontend-design` skill for the retention selector and numerals. I\'m aiming for a subtle, unboxed/segmented look with paired light/dark classes. No other skills are needed at this stage, as the task is to upgrade the pure TypeScript FSRS algorithm with React state management.\n\n\n**Verifying Algorithm Accuracy**\n\nI\'m now deeply immersed in the FSRS-4.5 formulas to ensure a mathematically faithful implementation. I\'m focusing on the Power Forgetting Curve, specifically the retrievability and optimal interval calculations. I\'m double-checking constants like `FACTOR` and `DECAY` to guarantee exact replication of the algorithm, paying close attention to the target retention calculation and the role of the FSRS-4.5 weights.\n\n\n**Calculating Initial Conditions**\n\nI\'m now calculating initial stability and difficulty based on grade values. I\'ve successfully implemented the formulas for $S_0(G)$ and $D_0(G)$ for new cards, including the clamping of difficulty. The initial values for the grades are confirmed, and now I\'m working on the update mechanisms for the algorithm. I\'m moving toward the difficulty updates, and stability after recall.\n\n\n**Defining State Transitions**\n\nI\'m now clarifying the state transitions and date formatting within the state machine, particularly for \"New\" cards. I\'m nailing down the precise logic for \"Learning\" and scheduled days. I\'m focusing on the implementation details for the \"Errei\" and \"Dif\303\255cil\" options, using a combination of `max` and `round` to schedule, and I\'m also addressing edge cases like intra-day and day-based schedules, considering the impact of the retention target.\n\n\n**Formatting Interval Estimates**\n\nI\'m now focusing on visually displaying interval estimates, dynamically adjusting to show hours or minutes when less than a day. I\'ve mapped desired retention percentages (85%, 90%, 95%) to recalculate interval estimations in real-time, providing concise, user-friendly feedback on each rating button. I\'m refining how learning and relearning steps are handled.\n\n\n**Implementing State Transitions**\n\nI\'m now implementing the state transitions based on user input. Specifically, I\'m defining the logic for each review option (\"Errei,\" \"Dif\303\255cil,\" \"Bom,\" \"F\303\241cil\") to accurately update stability, schedule the next review time, and manage lapsing. I\'m focusing on the interaction of the grade with new calculated stability values and ensuring the `scheduled_days` calculations are accurate. I\'m also ensuring the edge case for \"Errei\" is correctly implemented for a ten-minute interval. I\'m confident about these transition states!\n\nI\'m now also thinking about backward compatibility. To avoid breaking things for previous users, I\'m writing a `migrateCardToFsrs` normalizer function to handle legacy card data stored in `localStorage`. This will ensure seamless upgrades for existing users by correctly updating their data with any missing FSRS fields. I\'ll load any older cards with the normalizer in the `src/lib/srs.ts` file.\n\n\n**Defining Data Migrations**\n\nI\'m now implementing crucial data migration steps to ensure a smooth transition and no crashes. I\'m focusing on the `useMedR1Store.ts` file and its integration with `localStorage`. I\'m updating `src/types/medical.ts` to include FSRS-related fields and state management. I\'m also preparing for the incorporation of the full FSRS-4.5 engine in `src/lib/srs.ts`, alongside the critical seed data updates in `flashcardsSeed.ts`.\n\n\n**Integrating Retention Selector**\n\nI\'m now integrating the retention selector and interval display within the flashcard view, and making progress on updating the review buttons to dynamically reflect interval changes. I\'m working to expose and pass the target retention to the core FSRS functions. My focus is on the integration of the UI components and the FSRS logic.\n\n\n"
