    thinking: "**Analyzing the Request Details**\n\nI\'m now diving deep into the nuances of the user\'s request. My focus is on understanding the specifics of the FlashcardsView component and identifying the best way to incorporate the estimated review time. I am carefully considering the integration with FSRS and ensuring the widget or tooltip provides clear guidance.\n\n\n**Refining the Integration**\n\nI\'m now carefully examining the current implementation, specifically the existing `previewLabel` within the rating buttons. I\'ve pinpointed two key opportunities: an interactive FSRS forecast tooltip on hover/focus and a live summary strip widget. The aim is to provide users with rich, human-readable feedback, including Portuguese next review times, before they even click a rating button. The user experience must be improved with real-time stability and difficulty insights.\n\n\n**Evaluating Pre-Flip Visibility**\n\nI\'m now contemplating whether the \"Previs\303\243o FSRS\" widget should be visible even before the card is flipped. My instinct is leaning towards yes, ensuring users can glance at the card before making a decision. The objective is to facilitate FSRS-informed decisions from the get-go. I am carefully considering the best placement for the widget on the card.\n\n\n**Designing the UI Elements**\n\nI\'m now focusing on designing the user interface elements for FSRS integration. My plan involves a two-pronged approach: a discreet \"Previs\303\243o FSRS\" hover/click popover/tooltip button for interval previews and a more prominent FSRS Forecast Widget that appears immediately after card flipping. The widget will summarize intervals, include dynamic focus on the hovered rating button, and display stability and difficulty insights. Furthermore, each rating button will have a floating tooltip with detailed information. It will all be aligned with the design system.\n\n\n**Implementing the Formatting**\n\nI\'m now implementing the Portuguese interval formatting function, `formatFsrsIntervalLong`, to ensure clear and natural language for users. I\'m also planning to calculate complete `FsrsScheduleOutcome` objects within `FlashcardsView` to get richer data, including detailed due dates, stability, difficulty, and state. The plan is to get an exact next review date like \"26/11\" or \"Hoje, 15:52\".\n\n\n**Finalizing the Placement**\n\nI\'ve just finalized the placement and interactive design within `FlashcardsView.tsx`. Before the card flip, a clean `Previs\303\243o FSRS` info trigger (calendar-clock icon) in the footer offers a tooltip with the four intervals. After flipping, a streamlined FSRS Decision Widget appears above the rating buttons, showing a summary of intervals to facilitate quicker decision-making. The interaction design is now complete.\n\n\n**Implementing Hover Functionality**\n\nI\'m now implementing the hover functionality for the rating buttons, ensuring a smooth user experience. Upon hovering or focusing on a button, I\'ll display the precise FSRS forecast, including the colored indicator dot, the next review date, and stability/difficulty insights in a tabular format. Each button will also have a tooltip showing the interval and date. I\'m focusing on handling mouse events and focus states, syncing them with the hoveredRating state for a seamless transition.\n\n\n**Verifying Format Function**\n\nI\'m now meticulously verifying the edge cases within `formatFsrsIntervalLong` in `src/lib/srs.ts`, particularly for sub-day intervals when `hardIsSubDay` is true. My plan is to ensure sub-day intervals like \"10h\" are rendered accurately as \"10 horas\" or \"1 hora\" by directly incorporating long preview labels within the `FsrsScheduleOutcome`. This will ensure precise formatting consistency with due dates. I am focusing on lines 178-211 and 248-282.\n\n\n"
