  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Observing System Interaction**\n\nI\'m impressed by how the seeded flashcards and analytics are working together with this feature. Checking the cards due today, like `fc-q-usp-2025-cardio-01`, demonstrates strong accuracy within the \'Cardiologia\' set. The user has a 81.3% accuracy rate on the flashcard!\n\n\n**Calculating Target Retention**\n\nI\'ve determined target retention rates for several flashcards. For `fc-q-unicamp-2025-trauma-02`, the target is 92%. `fc-q-enare-2025-epi-07` yields a 94% target, highlighting high incidence and a point of attention, matching the example precisely. The next one, `fc-q-usp-2025-go-05` needs further data collection before a clear retention target can be generated.\n\n\n**Analyzing Incidence Rates**\n\nI\'m now diving into specific incidence rates across different exam boards. I\'m focusing on \"Alta incid\303\252ncia\" topics like Infectologia and Neonatologia within the UEPA board, adding 0.03 to the retention targets. For ENARE, I\'m mapping Pr\303\251-Natal to its corresponding weight (potentially 0.00). My goal is to fine-tune retention targets.\n\n\n**Evaluating Incidence Impact**\n\nI\'m currently assessing the impact of medium-incidence topics on retention. I\'ve classified areas like Pneumologia, Nefrologia, and Hematologia as medium-incidence, assigning them a base weight of 0.00. I\'m cross-referencing these classifications to tailor our retention targets for relevant flashcards to best reflect the medium incidence of the topics.\n\n\n**Classifying Incidences Further**\n\nI\'m now identifying low incidence topics and weighting them accordingly. Psiquiatria and Gastroenterologia are categorized as medium-incidence. Also, Reumatologia Rara, Subespecialidades Cir\303\272rgicas, Gen\303\251tica, and Oftalmologia/Otorrino have been identified as low incidence, and are given a -0.03 weight. For ENARE, I am evaluating topics of high incidence like Cardiologia, Pneumologia, Endocrinologia, Infectologia, Trauma and Abdome Agudo, Assist\303\252ncia ao Parto and S\303\255ndromes Hipertensivas, and giving them +0.03.\n\n\n**Refining Topic Weights**\n\nI\'m currently refining topic weights based on detailed incidence classifications. Now, I\'m assigning specific weights based on the exam. For topics like `Pr\303\251-ecl\303\242mpsia`, `Neonatologia` and `Puericultura` are being evaluated for ENARE and UEPA. For medium incidence topics, such as `Neurologia` and `Ortopedia`, a weight of 0.00 is assigned. Low-incidence areas like `Oncologia Pedi\303\241trica` and `Oftalmologia` are being considered for the -0.03 weight.\n\n\n**Determining Overall Incidences**\n\nI\'m now determining the overall incidence for the \"Geral\" profile. I\'m carefully considering a `deltaBanca` value for the general profile, which represents a balanced national residency profile. I am evaluating whether a default of `0.00` is best, representing standard incidence or if I should map core high-incidence topics to a value. The target is to find the best value for topics like Cardiologia and Trauma.\n\n\n**Defining Incidence for \"Geral\"**\n\nI\'m now carefully defining incidence weights for the \"Geral\" profile, which should represent a balanced national perspective. I\'m choosing between a +0.02 or +0.03 weight for common high-incidence topics, or if I should keep them neutral (0.00). My current thinking is a neutral 0.00 is best, especially when considering the ultra-rare subspecialties. For Geral, if someone selects it in Dynamic Calibration, they are aiming for national weights.\n\n\n**Establishing National Standards**\n\nI\'m now establishing national standards for all profiles, especially the \"GERAL\" profile, by defining the right weights. I\'ve opted for a streamlined +0.03, 0.00, -0.03 system to ensure consistent weight assignment, even with minor topic name variations. This should mean topics will inherit the right weight. I\'m exploring the implications for Dynamic Calibration.\n\n\n**Validating Board Differentiation**\n\nI\'m now validating board differentiation with the established weights. A system of +0.02, 0.00, and -0.02 has been implemented for high, medium, and low incidence topics. Switching between Geral (+0.02), UEPA (+0.03), and ENARE (0.00) for topics like Pneumologia and Obstetr\303\255cia produces significant and realistic differences across boards, demonstrating the effectiveness of the weights.\n\n\n**Implementing Partial Matching**\n\nI\'m implementing a smart partial-match fallback system. I am normalizing strings for accuracy, and prioritizing exact matches on canonical names. My code\'s matching logic prioritizes specific subtopic matches before broad area fallbacks, to ensure that rare topics receive appropriate attention. This will streamline the weight assignment process for various exam topics.\n\n\n**Refining Partial Matching**\n\nI\'m now refining the partial matching system. I\'ve focused on normalizing strings to improve accuracy, prioritizing exact matches, and defining fallback behavior. The new code prioritizes specific subtopic matches before broad area fallbacks, ensuring rare topics are accurately weighted. I am also exporting `DynamicRetentionBreakdown` and `calculateDynamicRetention` from `src/lib/srs.ts`, alongside associated interfaces.\n\n\n**Calculating Dynamic Retention**\n\nI\'m now calculating dynamic retention, focusing on `deltaIndividual`. If user accuracy and community median are available, I\'m finding the difference; also, I\'m handling the 0-100 percentage scale. I\'m incorporating `deltaIndividual` into the overall formula, ensuring its impact is appropriately reflected. My goal is to fine-tune the system\'s ability to provide more personalized retention targets.\n\n\n**Refining Dynamic Calculations**\n\nI\'m now refining the dynamic calculation logic, particularly focusing on `deltaIndividual`. If user accuracy data and the community median are present, I calculate the difference, scaling it to a 0\342\200\2231 fractional scale. I\'m adjusting retention based on performance relative to the median, providing personalized insights. I\'ve also clamped the result to be strictly between 0.85 and 0.95 and updated relevant functions.\n\n\n**Defining Retention Modes**\n\nI\'m now defining retention modes, focusing on fixed and dynamic options. I\'ve created the `FixedTargetRetention` type as `0.85 | 0.9 | 0.95`, and `RetentionMode` as either `\'dinamica\'` or the `FixedTargetRetention` options. I\'m choosing to keep `TargetRetention = number` for mathematical flexibility. I\'ve also defined the `ExamBoard` type for clarity. I\'m making sure TypeScript is type-safe and clean.\n\n\n\n**Designing Retention Modes**\n\nI\'m now designing retention modes, considering both fixed and dynamic options. I\'ve defined `FixedTargetRetention` with values 0.85, 0.9, and 0.95. I\'m choosing `\'dinamica\'` as a `RetentionMode` or fixed values. I\'ve also defined `ExamBoard` for improved type safety. I\'m also planning to implement `userAccuracyByTopic` and `communityMedianByTopic` directly within the store.\n\n\n\n**Implementing Retention Control**\n\nI\'m now implementing the FlashcardsView.tsx changes, adding toolbar controls for `Banca-Alvo` and `Reten\303\247\303\243o FSRS`. I\'ve added a compact segmented control for `UEPA`, `ENARE`, and `Geral`, persisting the `Banca-Alvo` selection in local storage. Also, I\'ve integrated a segmented control for `Din\303\242mica`, `85%`, `90%`, and `95%` retention, with local storage persistence. The logic for dynamic vs. fixed retention mode is now operational, including a visual indicator for dynamic mode.\n\n\n**Displaying Calculated Retention**\n\nI\'m now implementing the display of calculated retention. Inside the active review card, there will be the retention percentage and its justification, depending on dynamic or fixed mode. In dynamic mode, I\'ll show details like incidence, exam board adjustments and the user\'s accuracy vs. the median. In fixed mode, I will show what\'s being recommended. I\'ll also add this same calculated retention into the FSRS forecast panel header.\n\n\n"
