  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Medical Questions**\n\nI\'ve just dived into the demo question bank and noticed a key detail. There are 18 medical questions overall. Crucially, 16 are active, and 2 are outdated. All these questions fall under the \"GERAL\" category, with more than 10 questions. This means the Pareto 80/20 calculation is already live, running directly from this real-world question bank!\n\n\n**Refining Pareto Calculations**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the Pareto engine\'s implementation, aiming for both mathematical precision and clear demonstration. I see that ENARE has 4 questions, UEPA has 1 question, and USP-SP has 3. Because these all fall under the threshold of 10, I can use this to demonstrate the intended fallback behavior in the demo data.\n\n\n**Calculating Cumulative Percentages**\n\nI\'m currently working through the cumulative percentage calculations for each topic. I\'m focusing on the logic for the Pareto tier assignments, specifically the 80% boundary. I\'m making sure that the topic crossing 80% is correctly assigned to the \"N\303\272cleo Pareto\" tier, and that any subsequent topics are properly classified. The plan is to have a robust method to determine cumulative percentages and ensure precise tier assignments.\n\n\n**Verifying Tier Assignments**\n\nI\'m now tracing the GERAL topic with its 18 questions across 14 themes, double checking tier assignments, especially the 80% boundary. I\'m carefully observing how each theme\'s cumulative percentage impacts the Pareto tier designation. I want to confirm the \"N\303\272cleo Pareto\" assignment is accurate when we cross 80%.\n\n\n**Confirming Threshold Logic**\n\nI\'m now carefully examining the precise logic that determines the Pareto tier assignments. Specifically, I\'m verifying the 80% and 95% boundaries for the \"Alta,\" \"M\303\251dia,\" and \"Baixa\" tiers. I\'m focusing on the condition where the cumulative percentage exceeds 80%, using the new data to ensure the proper assignment of items to the \"Alta\" tier. It all looks very clean!\n\n\n**Refining Tier Criteria**\n\nI\'ve streamlined the tier assignment logic significantly. My focus is now on ensuring accurate tier assignments for varying cumulative percentages, especially around the 80% and 95% thresholds. I\'ve traced the logic through the GERAL dataset, seeing each tier\342\200\224Alta, M\303\251dia, and Baixa\342\200\224accurately assigned with clean cutoffs. The 80% Pareto divider works flawlessly. I\'m satisfied!\n\n\n**Implementing Fallback Logic**\n\nI\'m now implementing the fallback behavior for datasets with fewer than 10 questions. The plan is to retain the real data statistics from the existing questions, but also supply a fallback Pareto distribution based on the default matrix weights. This approach ensures transparency while still providing a usable Pareto analysis. Even when there\'s insufficient data, I am now able to return both the actual topic frequencies and a fallback, estimated Pareto distribution. I\'m focusing on providing all the data, including a boolean to indicate fallback usage.\n\n\n**Clarifying Fallback Details**\n\nI am now focusing on the nuances of the fallback behavior for datasets lacking sufficient questions. I want to make sure the presentation clearly distinguishes between live data and the default matrix weights. I\'ll maintain the real data, even when normalized, and highlight any fallback implementation with a visual cue, such as a banner, and then report the boolean flag showing fallback usage.\n\n\n**Visualizing Fallback Details**\n\nI am now focusing on the presentation for fallback scenarios, particularly the Pareto curve and its visual elements across all tabs. I want the **Corte Pareto (80%)** divider to display cleanly, even when using the board\'s matrix. I\'m ensuring both the live data and the fallback Pareto analysis are clearly presented, with recalculation options. The banner, flag, and visuals are coming together, providing a clear experience.\n\n\n**Integrating Exam Weights**\n\nI\'m now integrating the Pareto calculations with the FSRS system. The `getExamTopicWeight` function will prioritize custom and manual weights first. Then, for the Pareto results, I am planning to integrate the `calculateParetoByExam` results, as long as the board contains enough data to perform the calculations. I\'m making sure it all fits together, and am now incorporating data from both the board and the question bank!\n\n\n**Aligning Topic Weights**\n\nI\'m now integrating Pareto-based exam weight recalculation. For boards with sufficient data (>= 10 questions), I\'ll align topic weights based on the Pareto 80/20 rule, overriding any manual adjustments. For datasets below the threshold, the function `applyParetoToBoardWeights` ensures a fallback to the default matrix. I\'m making sure it all fits together, and am now incorporating data from both the board and the question bank!\n\n\n**Testing Recalculation Behavior**\n\nI\'m verifying the recalculation logic in the scenario where manual adjustments are made, specifically using the \"Recalcular pelo Pareto do Banco\" button. The recalculation from the 18 bank questions for \"Cardiologia\" works, and the FSRS is instantly updated. Furthermore, I\'ve checked and confirmed proper fallback behavior with the  `ENARE` board, which has fewer than 10 questions.\n\n\n**Verifying Weight Integration**\n\nI\'m now confirming the seamless integration of FSRS with Pareto exam weight calculation and display of the `ExamWeightsModal.tsx` UI enhancements. I\'ve verified the `R_alvo` clamp logic and checked for every visual detail, including individual and cumulative percentages next to each topic, plus the micro-bars, the horizontal divider line, and Pareto order. Everything aligns perfectly!\n\n\n**Designing Visual Dividers**\n\nI\'m now focusing on visual cues to enhance the clarity of the Pareto analysis within the user interface. I want to highlight the 80% cutoff with a dashed divider line and a label, improving scannability. For boards with sufficient questions, I\'ll include a recalculation button, along with a Pareto status bar.\n\n\n**Finalizing UI Integration**\n\nI\'m now putting the finishing touches on the user interface integration. I am implementing the automatic calculation, weight updates, version bumps, and clear feedback mechanisms, as well as checking and guaranteeing zero circular imports. I am making sure the logic for data loading, parameterization, and dependency injection is correct. I am finalizing the Pareto implementation!\n\n\n"
