  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing User Requirements**\n\nI\'m meticulously reviewing the user prompt to ensure a flawless implementation. I\'m starting by dissecting each requirement, making notes, so I don\'t miss a beat. Currently, I am working with the specified filename and function name. Next I will make sure to check the others!\n\n\n**Refining Pareto Calculations**\n\nI\'m now streamlining the Pareto calculations for exams. The plan is to export `calculateExamPareto` from `src/lib/paretoCalculator.ts`, which will call existing functions like `calculateParetoByExam` and `buildBoardParetoProfile` with full board matrix support. This also will enable `ParetoChart.tsx` to directly connect to `calculateExamPareto`. I\'m also considering `calculateExamPareto(board: ExamBoard, questions?: MedicalQuestion[])` as an alias or wrapper and a dedicated chart helper to handle topics and area sorting.\n\n\n**Defining Topic Attributes**\n\nI\'m deep in the weeds defining the attributes for each topic. I need to nail down details like the full name, a concise short name for axis labels, and its incidence percentage. I am also calculating the cumulative Pareto percentage and designating whether it\'s part of the Pareto core. Matching student accuracy to topic data is another piece I am working on.\n\n\n**Calculating ROI for Pareto Charts**\n\nI\'m currently calculating the Return on Investment (ROI) score. It considers incidence percentage and student accuracy, using a formula: `incidencePct * Math.max(0, 100 - (studentAccuracyPct ?? 65)) / 100`. High incidence with low student accuracy gives the highest ROI. I am also working on creating the `ParetoChart.tsx` component, which will use `recharts` for visualization. I need to add a header and a board selector. I\'m prioritizing adding the `An\303\241lise de Pareto (80/20) por Banca` title and subtitle, and am working on clean color dots for the legend.\n\n\n**Visualizing Chart Elements**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the chart\'s visual elements. I\'m assigning specific colors to different categories: the Pareto core, the range outside the 80% cut-off, and the cumulative curve. I\'m also mapping axes, including a right Y-axis for cumulative Pareto percentage and a left Y-axis for incidence percentage. I\'m building bars with color highlighting based on incidence and student accuracy.\n\n\n**Refining Chart Aesthetics**\n\nI\'m now refining the chart\'s visual style. I\'m focusing on color-coding for categories like Pareto core and areas outside the 80% cut-off. I\'m also ensuring the correct axis mappings and building the bars with dynamic color highlights based on incidence and accuracy. Additionally, I am styling the tooltip to accurately show the full topic name, incidence, accumulated Pareto, student accuracy, and a status tag. I am including a note about bank sample sizes.\n\n\n\n**Integrating ROI Module**\n\nI\'m integrating the ROI module into the Pareto analysis. I am creating a horizontal strip within the card to show the top 3 topics by ROI. Each card will display rank, topic name, key metrics like incidence, accuracy, and potential gain, along with buttons to access related questions and flashcards. I am now working on the navigation callbacks for the action buttons within the ROI cards, ensuring the functionality smoothly filters the question bank and flashcards.\n\n\n**Defining Action Button Callbacks**\n\nI\'m now implementing the navigation callbacks for the action buttons. If a topic matches a question theme, I\'ll filter by that theme and area. If the topic is broader, I\'ll search for the core keyword to ensure relevant question results, resetting the simulated exam. Likewise, when filtering flashcards, I\'ll pass initial filter requests into the `FlashcardsView`. I\'ve added a note to inspect and replace the end of `/src/lib/paretoCalculator.ts`.\n\n\n"
