  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Designing the Projection Engine**\n\nI\'m focusing intently on designing the projection engine, particularly the calculation for estimating days to achieve 95% retention in high-incidence topics, using a clean, transparent, and responsive approach. This involves carefully considering the math and domain model within `src/lib/srs.ts`.\n\n\n**Refining Accuracy Calculation**\n\nI\'m now refining how the accuracy component influences the 95% retention projection. I\'m focusing on the interplay between high-incidence flashcards, the user\'s current retention in those topics, and their daily review cadence, ensuring the calculation handles both reviewed and new cards gracefully. Specifically, I\'m integrating the FSRS retrievability for reviewed cards, and user accuracy or baseline, if not yet consolidated, to reflect their current high-incidence progress.\n\n\n**Calculating Review Cadence**\n\nI\'m now incorporating actual review activity to refine the daily card review pace. I\'m leveraging `reviewsTodayStats.total` and recent review history to infer the user\'s empirical cadence. This is crucial for a realistic projection. I\'m also implementing a baseline when no reviews have occurred today, based on recent averages or a minimum sensible value, ensuring a meaningful projection even on initial load, which will adjust dynamically as the user reviews cards.\n\n\n**Estimating Consolidation Passes**\n\nI\'m now determining the remaining successful review cycles needed for each card, based on its state and stability. This includes weighting the effort by the user\'s current accuracy in high-incidence topics, using a formula that reflects how lower accuracy translates to more lapses. Also, I\'m integrating the spacing calendar floor to respect FSRS consolidation requirements for new or weak cards.\n\n\n"
    message_id: "wjQCa8jBKdre-sAPmYShqQY"
