  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Interface Design**\n\nI\'m currently focused on dissecting the UI/UX design of a medical residency exam platform from Brazil. The reference screenshot reveals a remarkably clean and modern interface, which I\'m categorizing under `frontend-design` for analysis.\n\n\n**Examining Design Inspiration**\n\nI\'m now diving deeper into the referenced UI, focusing specifically on how its \"Question Solver\" achieves clarity. The clean header strip is catching my eye; it\'s efficiently organized with a back button, question counter, score, and a \"Finalizar\" option. Beneath that, there\'s a segmented progress bar. I am comparing it to our Question Solver, and identifying improvements we can incorporate, all within MedR1\'s existing constraints.\n\n\n**Refining Question Navigation**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the exam interface\'s question navigation elements. I envision a crisp, segmented progress bar (10 segments!), replacing the current numbered buttons, with color-coded segments to indicate question status. Each segment will be clickable, allowing instant navigation. Below that, I\'m conceptualizing a \"Utility Tools Row\" with streamlined specialty/difficulty metadata and three icon-buttons for highlighting/strikethrough, clinical hints, and annotations.\n\n\n**Evaluating Interface Improvements**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the exam interface\'s help popover, which features keyboard shortcuts (`A-E`, `Enter`, `\342\206\220/\342\206\222`, etc.), along with bookmark and flag actions. I\'m also examining the ultra-clean enunciado & alternative cards, and I\'m especially interested in the inline, expandable \"Ver coment\303\241rio\" toggle within each alternative card, which is a significant UX improvement over our current format. I\'m taking note of the colored indicators for correct/incorrect answers. Furthermore, I am considering incorporating a floating/compact drawer button for a map.\n\n\n"
